Deep dive: roadwork =E2=86=92 bus causal outcome Exact source stack

*Street Manager Event API*

   - Official guidance:
   https://www.gov.uk/guidance/find-and-use-roadworks-data
   - API surface includes permit/activity event_reference, event_type,
   work/activity reference, coordinates, proposed and actual dates, status,
   traffic-management type, USRN, event_time, object_reference, and version=
.
   - Current v7 sandbox Swagger exposes GET /works/updates, POST
   /permits/search, plus /api-notifications/subscribe, /unsubscribe, and
   /subscriptions.
   - Public roadworks data is free to view via API; account/API access is
   the realistic permission path.

*Bus Open Data Service (BODS)*

   - Official guidance:
   https://www.gov.uk/guidance/find-and-use-bus-open-data
   - Local bus timetable/location/fares data in England is freely available
   for commercial and non-commercial reuse.
   - Vehicle locations use SIRI-VM. Operators must publish location updates
   every 10=E2=80=9330 seconds; the profile contains VehicleJourneyRef, Veh=
icleRef,
   RecordedAtTime, coordinates and journey/call information.
   - API consumers register for an account/key. DfT warns abusive overuse
   can lead to access being switched off, so POW should use a bounded regio=
nal
   collector first.

Canonical identity graph

StreetManager.object_reference/version -> USRN + geometry -> affected road
segment -> route geometry -> VehicleJourneyRef -> VehicleRef(day-scoped) ->
scheduled calls -> observed SIRI-VM trajectory

Every edge stores status =E2=88=88 {CONFIRMED, INFERRED, UNKNOWN}, confiden=
ce,
method, source, observed_at, and raw provenance.

Important identity semantics:

   - object_reference + version =3D intervention version identity.
   - USRN is the stable street join but geometry is required because one
   USRN can cover more road than the actual works footprint.
   - VehicleJourneyRef is the preferred operational journey key and is
   defined to correspond to timetable identity.
   - VehicleRef must be consistent through the day, but should not be
   assumed to be a permanent physical-bus identity across days without
   additional evidence.

Temporal event schema

ROADWORK_PROPOSED, ROADWORK_UPDATED, ROADWORK_STARTED, ROADWORK_CANCELLED,
ROADWORK_ENDED, TRAFFIC_MANAGEMENT_CHANGED, BUS_ENTERED_EXPOSURE,
BUS_EXITED_EXPOSURE, DELAY_DELTA, RECOVERY.

Fields: entity_key, event_type, effective_at, observed_at, source_version,
raw_hash, confidence, provenance.
First proprietary transformations

RoadworkImpact(work_type, traffic_management, road_segment, time)

ExcessJourneySeconds =3D observed segment time - matched counterfactual
segment time

RecoveryHalfLife =3D time after intervention end until journey performance
returns halfway to baseline

InterventionReliability =3D P(actual disruption | proposed work attributes)

Eventually: ExpectedDisruptionCost(action, place, time) and
BestInterventionWindow.
Outcome acquisition

No private/person-level data required. Outcomes are public operational
consequences: journey time, lateness, cancellation/absence proxy, recovery.
A later commercial layer can accept consenting fleet/local-authority
outcome data, but it is not required to plant the garden.
Smallest coverage test

Start with one dense English urban authority for 24 hours.

   1. Subscribe/poll Street Manager updates and preserve every observed
   version.
   2. Select works with precise geometry + USRN + actual timing.
   3. Resolve bus routes intersecting a buffered works geometry.
   4. Capture affected SIRI-VM journeys plus matched same-route/time
   controls.
   5. Compute segment traversal and schedule deviation before/during/after.

